sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch15-10> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Under constant pressure the wall thickness of a blood-vessel should vary as its radius (P = T/R); because pressure falls with friction from large to small arteries, wall thickness is proportional to vessel size in larger vessels but becomes disproportionately thin in small arteries and capillaries.";
    dc:type "mathematical-law";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Vessel wall thickness tracks radius in large arteries but falls off in small ones";
    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#notation> "P = T/R; at constant P, wall thickness t \\propto R";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter XV, “On the Shapes of Eggs, and of Certain Other Hollow Structures”; topic “Blood-vessel walls”. Scope: arteries and capillaries of vertebrates"
}
